        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Real-time tracking of crewmember location provides valuable data in case of contingencies requiring fast awareness by other crewmembers and ground support.  Historical logging of crewmember location supports analyses for streamlining operations and habitat layout, as well as supporting synchronization of location with crew-worn environmental exposure sensors.
<P>
The objective of this experiment was to evaluate the performance, ease of setup, and ease of use of two crew location tracking systems that are based on two different sensor technologies, infrared (IR) and radio frequency (RF).  
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
The IR and RF technologies have different susceptibilities that can limit receipt of signal/data, and therefore drive specific configurations of transmitters and receivers.  The Versus Information System (VIS) used IR transmitter badges worn by crewmembers and a wired network of fixed-location infrared sensors to detect the badges.  As the infrared signal required line-of-sight, sensor placement was dictated by the position of physical barriers.  The Ekahau Positioning Engine (EPE) used RF signal transmissions via 802.11b between the crew worn personal data assistants (PDAs)(the iPAQ 5555 in this case), wireless access points (WAP) deployed about the habitat, and the laptop receiver.  While the RF signal does not require line-of-sight, it was deflected by the module’s metal walls.  This multi-pathing influenced the positioning of the WAPs and laptop receiver.
<P>
For each technology, the crewmembers received an initial layout for the placement of IR sensors or WAPs, and the laptop receiver.  Crewmembers performed checkout and verification of signal receipt from each sensor.  Sensor relocation was performed if needed.  After checkout, the crew performed a system calibration
<P>
For each technology, the crewmembers performed the following:<BR>
1.  Deploy the IR sensors or WAPs plus the laptop receiver per the provided initial layout.<BR>
2. Checkout the system to verify receipt of signal from each sensor. If signal was not received, perform a relocation procedure as required.<BR>
3. Perform a system calibration.<BR>
4. Perform and log a pre-defined walk about the habitat.
<P>
As time and batteries were available, the systems continued to record crew movement in the background of other experiments.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Crewmembers reported that setting up the sensors and performing a system calibration test were very easy. However, they determined that the badge is probably not the best for microgravity.  Additionally, crewmembers offered suggestions for securing the worn sensor.<END>
        </div>

